      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sudden acidity spikes often suggest the yeast or bacteria are overactive, which temperature or flour can affect. Try lowering your ambient kitchen temperature slightly to slow down fermentation without completely stopping it. Alternatively, switching to a slightly higher protein flour can provide more stable food for the microbes, helping to regulate the activity. Monitor your starter closely over a few feedings to see which adjustment yields more consistent rise and flavor.",
